158
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.440
So even though we shot this in October, it was going to air in like January or February.
所以尽管我们是在十月拍摄的，但它会在一月或二月左右播出。

159
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:21.900
It would be winter in Scranton.
那时斯克兰顿正值冬季。

160
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.340
And I love it.
我太喜欢这个设定了。

161
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.960
I love that throughout the episode, you see people like having talking heads, and it's just coming down.
我喜欢这一整集里，当你看到人们在进行访谈时，外面一直在下雪。

162
00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.520
I just love that little detail.
我就是喜欢这种小细节。

163
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.820
Well, here is what we did to make it snow in those talking heads, Angela.
好吧，安吉拉，这就是我们让访谈画面里下雪的方法。

164
00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:40.880
We used the same company that we used in the Christmas episode, the first Christmas episode.
我们用了和拍摄圣诞特辑时同一家公司，就是第一集圣诞特辑那次。

165
00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:41.460
Remember them?
还记得他们吗？

166
00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:42.220
Snow business?
“雪之生意”公司？

167
00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:43.020
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

168
00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.300
And they used this very old-fashioned technique.
他们用了一种非常老派的技术。

169
00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.400
He said they've been using it for decades, and they still use it today.
他说他们几十年来一直都在用这个方法，直到今天还在用。

170
00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.960
They take like a large roller, and they put it behind the window.
他们拿一个大滚筒，把它放在窗户后面。

171
00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.140
And it has like, what's the thing that you put your pasta in to drain it?
就像那种用来沥干意面的漏勺一样？

172
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.680
Or like a sifter when you do powdered sugar?
或者像撒糖粉用的筛子？

173
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.040
Yes.
对。

174
00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:11.080
So it's a giant rolling sifter that they fill with snow, and then they just crank it.
所以那是一个巨大的旋转筛子，他们往里面装满雪，然后不停地摇动把手。

175
00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.780
There's like a dude back there cranking fake snow like through a giant sifter?
所以后面有个家伙在摇把手，让假雪通过那个巨大的筛子落下来？

176
00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.620
Yeah, there can be a dude or a lady, or it can be on a little motor.
对，可以是男的也可以是女的，或者装个小马达也行。

177
00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:25.820
It just spins endlessly, and snow falls out of it until you fill it again.
它会不停地旋转，雪就会一直落下来，直到需要重新加雪为止。

178
00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.420
And he even sent me a picture of it, so I will put it up on our Office Ladies Pod Instagram.
他甚至还给我发了照片，我会把它发到我们的《办公室女士》播客Instagram上。

179
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.620
Oh, I want to see that picture.
哦，我一定要看看那张照片。

180
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:32.980
Yeah.
好的。

181
00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.060
And then this same company, Snow Business, would come and put all the snow and ice on the ground,
然后这家“雪之生意”公司还会过来，把所有的雪和冰铺在地上，

182
00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:42.220
and they had this big blower machine where they would like blow it all over the bushes.
他们还有一台大型吹雪机，可以把雪吹得到处都是，覆盖在灌木丛上。

183
00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:48.200
I remember the blower machine, but I don't think I ever went back behind where the windows were
我记得那台吹雪机，但我好像从来没去过窗户后面，

184
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.980
where we did talking heads, and I never got to see the sifter.
也就是我们拍访谈的地方，所以我从没见过那个筛子。

185
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.560
Well, there's a little gap, you know?
嗯，那里有个小缝隙，你知道吧？

186
00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.480
There's like the wall, and then there's a gap, and then there's that Scranton backdrop
就是墙后面有个空隙，然后是那个斯克兰顿的背景板，

187
00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.720
that we would also change seasonally.
我们也会根据季节更换背景板。

188
00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:03.860
So we had a snowy backdrop, and then we had a little sifter fella.
所以我们有雪景背景板，还有一个小筛子装置。

189
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:04.820
Our sifter person.
我们的筛雪专员。

190
00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.060
Yeah, exactly.
没错，正是如此。

191
00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.240
Well, I love that fast fact.
好吧，我喜欢这个速报小知识。

192
00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.540
Well, fast fact number two, Angela, remember last week we talked about how this episode
那么速报小知识之二，安吉拉，还记得上周我们提到这一集

193
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.900
and Traveling Salesman were shot as one big episode?
和《旅行推销员》是作为一整集拍摄的吗？

194
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.640
And our director of this episode and our showrunner, Greg Daniels, sent in a bunch of audio clips
我们这一集的导演兼剧集运作人格雷格·丹尼尔斯发来了一些音频片段，

195
00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.180
where he mentioned some of the inspiration for these two episodes.
他在里面提到了这两集的部分灵感来源。

196
00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:31.020
Well, he did it again, and he sent in an audio clip about some of the things that they
他又发来了一段音频，讲了一些他们

197
00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.920
were juggling for this portion of the storyline.
在处理这部分剧情时所面临的挑战。

198
00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:35.500
So Sam, will you play that clip?
萨姆，能播放一下那段音频吗？

199
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.820
These were like extremely complex storylines.
这些剧情线极其复杂。

200
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.000
There was a lot of good stuff between Pam and Angela, between Dwight and Angela, between
帕姆和安吉拉之间，德怀特和安吉拉之间，还有……之间有很多精彩的内容。

201
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:43.960
Michael and Dwight.
迈克尔和德怀特。

202
00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:51.160
And these stories brought in lots of cards that we had on the boards.
这些故事带来了很多我们在板子上贴的卡片。

203
00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.820
One of the things that we would do in pre-production is we would generate all these funny ideas
我们在前期制作时会做的一件事，就是构思出各种有趣的创意，

204
00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:05.380
cards for different storylines or fragments or B stories, and they would become note cards
为不同的故事情节、片段或副线故事写下卡片，它们会变成笔记卡，

205
00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:09.400
that would be pushpinned into the cork boards.
用图钉钉在软木板上。

206
00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:11.720
So there's a lot of cards.
所以卡片非常多。

207
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.660
I recognize cards in the stories that had been on the boards.
我认出了板子上那些故事里的卡片。

208
00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:22.700
I think the Mexican-themed party, for instance, was a card for Oscar.
比如，我觉得墨西哥主题派对就是奥斯卡的卡片。

209
00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:32.440
And certainly the Pam and Angela relationship or Andy being more sinister and Michael having
当然还有帕姆和安吉拉的关系，或者安迪变得更阴险，以及迈克尔不得不

210
00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:39.120
to wise up and realize who his most loyal servant was.
变得聪明起来，意识到谁才是他最忠诚的仆人。

211
00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:47.540
These are all fragmentary ideas that we combined into a great episode, I think.
这些都是零碎的想法，我认为我们把它们组合成了一集很棒的剧集。

212
00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.140
A lot of moving parts.
有很多变动的部分。

213
00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:50.980
Yeah.
是的。

214
00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:55.080
I love picturing the writer's room in these cards and them coming back around.
我喜欢想象编剧室里这些卡片的样子，以及它们如何再次出现。

215
00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.100
And, Greg, I love when you send in audio clips.
还有，格雷格，我喜欢你发来的音频片段。

216
00:10:58.300 --> 00:10:58.720
Thank you.
谢谢。

217
00:10:58.840 --> 00:10:59.300
I agree.
我同意。

218
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.420
Fast fact number three.
冷知识第三条。

219
00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:03.540
Rid-a-dip-da-doo.
Rid-a-dip-da-doo。

220
00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:05.740
Ed HelMs.
艾德·赫尔姆斯。

221
00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:07.960
You heard her, everybody.
大家都听到了吧。

222
00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.540
We talked to Ed Helms about this episode.
我们和艾德·赫尔姆斯聊了这一集。

223
00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.780
This is such a big episode for Andy.
这对安迪来说是非常重要的一集。

224
00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.220
He is driving most of the storyline.
他推动了大部分的故事情节。

225
00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:16.580
He is the instigator.
他是始作俑者。

226
00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.280
We absolutely had to talk to Ed, so we did.
我们绝对必须和艾德聊聊，所以我们聊了。

227
00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:19.920
And it was great.
而且感觉很棒。

228
00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:20.780
It was so fun.
太有趣了。

229
00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:21.900
Sam, play the interview.
萨姆，播放采访录音。

230
00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:33.320
Helms, hello.
赫尔姆斯，你好。

231
00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.620
How are you?
你好吗？

232
00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:36.500
Hi, office ladies.
嗨，办公室女士们。

233
00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:38.800
Ed, it's so good to see you.
艾德，见到你真好。

234
00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.700
You got an office guy right here in the mix.
我们这儿正好有一位办公室成员。

235
00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:42.040
We do.
确实。

236
00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:43.340
We got an office fella.
我们有一位办公室伙计。

237
00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.760
We got one of the best office fellas.
我们有最棒的办公室伙计之一。

238
00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:46.200
Aw.
噢。

239
00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:46.600
Aw.
噢。

240
00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.740
Well, thanks so much for being here, Ed.
好吧，非常感谢你能来，艾德。

241
00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:54.820
People just love Andy Bernard, and we're so excited to talk to you about this episode.
大家都很喜欢安迪·伯纳德，我们很高兴能和你聊聊这一集。

242
00:11:54.820 --> 00:12:05.640
Yeah, I rewatched a little bit just so I had it in my head for this chat, because I never watch it, unless I happen to see it on in the background somewhere.
是啊，我重看了一点，好让我在这次聊天时能有点印象，因为我从不看它，除非碰巧在什么地方的背景里看到。

243
00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.860
But I love it when I catch it.
但我很喜欢偶尔看到它的感觉。

244
00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:08.820
Yeah.
是的。

245
00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:09.380
Yeah.
是的。

246
00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:13.980
Have you ever had that experience, though, where you're, like, flying on an airplane,
不过，你有没有过那种经历，比如你正在坐飞机，

247
00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:22.900
and you see it on all the screens that people are watching it on all their personal devices and on the airplane, and then you're just sort of sitting there.
然后你看到人们在各自的个人设备和飞机屏幕上都在看这部剧，而你只是坐在那儿。

248
00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.700
Yeah, please don't notice me.
是啊，求求你们别注意到我。

249
00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:25.720
Please don't notice me.
求求你们别注意到我。

250
00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.060
Yeah, I'm always like, do I go to the bathroom?
是啊，我总是想，我是不是该去趟洗手间？

251
00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:29.620
Do I not go to the bathroom?
我难道不能去洗手间吗？

252
00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:38.960
I was trying to time it out to go to the bathroom one time, and, like, there was someone in there, so I was standing outside, and I was on the little screen above my head.
有一次我试着算好时间去洗手间，结果里面有人，我就站在外面，抬头看到了头顶的小屏幕。

253
00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:39.800
Oh, my God.
天哪。

254
00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.240
And I just started to see the faces.
然后我就开始看到那些面孔。

255
00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:41.760
Look at me.
看着我。

256
00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:42.180
Look up.
抬头看。

257
00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:42.600
Look at me.
看着我。

258
00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:43.000
Look up.
抬头看。

259
00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:43.760
And I was like, oh, God.
当时我就想，天哪。

260
00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:45.720
Oh, that's amazing.
噢，那太棒了。

261
00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:50.520
Angela, I would expect you to just sit down on someone's armrest and be like, hey, you having fun?
安吉拉，我倒是觉得你会直接坐在别人的扶手上说，嘿，玩得开心吗？

262
00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:51.540
You enjoying the episode?
这集看得过瘾吗？

263
00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:52.540
Well, you got any questions?
有什么问题吗？

264
00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.720
I'm happy to talk about it.
我很乐意聊聊。

265
00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:59.760
I've exchanged more email addresses with flight attendants and made good friends on planes.
我跟空乘人员交换过不少邮箱地址，还在飞机上交到了好朋友。

266
00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.340
Literally, I sat next to a woman one time, and we're still friends to this day.
真的，有一次我坐在一个女人旁边，我们至今还是朋友。

267
00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.620
We're total strangers flying to Canada.
我们当时完全是陌生人，一起飞往加拿大。

268
00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.100
So, Ed, you watched part of The Return?
所以，埃德，你看了《回归》那部分了吗？

269
00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:09.240
Yeah.
看了。

270
00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:11.200
Yeah, I re-watched The Return.
是的，我重温了《回归》。

271
00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:20.100
You know, I watched just a couple of episodes from that first chunk of season three because that was my first time on the show.
你知道，我只看了第三季开头的那几集，因为那是我第一次上节目。

272
00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:20.620
Yeah.
是啊。

273
00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:25.260
And it feels so long ago.
感觉那已经是好久以前的事了。

274
00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:30.880
I can't – like, it's just not fresh in my mind, so many of those experiences.
我没法——就是说，很多经历在我的脑海里已经不新鲜了。

275
00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:40.480
And so, re-watching it, I was hoping I could kind of trigger a lot of memories of kind of the experience and the emotional memories and so forth.
所以重温的时候，我希望能借此唤起很多关于那段经历的记忆，还有情感记忆等等。

276
00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:42.880
And it was a joy.
那真是一种享受。

277
00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:44.440
It was really, really fun.
真的非常非常有意思。

278
00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.760
When I watch it, I get a sense memory of my desk.
当我观看的时候，我会产生一种关于我办公桌的感官记忆。

279
00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:48.560
Hmm.
嗯。

280
00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:54.880
I know that they've torn it down now, and they do a lot of game shows where our set used to be.
我知道他们现在已经把它拆了，而且在我们的旧片场拍了很多游戏节目。

281
00:13:55.020 --> 00:14:07.260
And I was even invited to go on one of them, and I turned it down because I didn't think that I could emotionally handle walking into the soundstage and not seeing Pam's desk.
我甚至还被邀请去参加其中一个，但我拒绝了，因为我觉得自己无法在情感上接受走进那个摄影棚却看不到帕姆办公桌的场景。

282
00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:08.280
Wow.
哇。

283
00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:08.700
Weird.
真奇怪。

284
00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:13.000
At Chandler Valley Center Studios, they do game shows now?
在钱德勒谷中心制片厂，他们现在拍游戏节目吗？

285
00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:13.500
Yeah.
是的。

286
00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:13.600
Yes.
没错。

287
00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.980
I did, to tell the truth.
说实话，我确实答应了。

288
00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.640
And when I got the address, I said, yes, I would do it.
当我拿到地址时，我说，好，我会参加的。

289
00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.280
And, you know, I don't know where things tape.
而且，你知道，我不知道节目是在哪里录制的。

290
00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:21.520
So, I was like, yeah, sure, I'll do it.
所以当时我就想，好啊，没问题，我会去的。

291
00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:25.540
And then I got the address, and it was Chandler Valley Studios.
然后我收到了地址，发现是钱德勒谷制片厂。

292
00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:26.660
And I was like, what?
当时我就想，什么？

293
00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:30.040
I showed up at our studio, and I started sobbing.
我到了我们的制片厂，然后就开始大哭。

294
00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:30.860
Oh.
噢。

295
00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.380
Because it was just so weird to pull up to that building.
因为开车到那栋楼前感觉真是太奇怪了。

296
00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:35.580
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

297
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:46.000
And there were a lot of, you know, there were a lot of major chapters in our lives that people went through.
而且你知道，我们生活中经历过很多重要的篇章。

298
00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:52.820
Marriages, divorces, children, all these kind of major life events that were happening.
结婚、离婚、生子，所有这些重大的人生事件都在发生。

299
00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:57.620
And sometimes I'll see an episode, and I'll be like, oh, yeah, that's when so-and-so was going through this.
有时我看到某一集，就会想，噢，对了，那是某某正在经历这些的时候。

300
00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:01.620
Or that's when so-and-so child was born.
或者那是某某的孩子出生的时候。

301
00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:06.760
And that stuff's really beautiful to remember.
那些回忆真的很美好。

302
00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:16.240
Even the parts that might have been painful chapters, they're still merely meaningful chapters in our lives and histories.
即使是那些可能令人痛苦的篇章，它们依然是我们人生和历史中充满意义的章节。

303
00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:21.180
And it's just so wild to have this show that's kind of a bookmark, right?
能有这样一部剧作为一种书签，感觉真的很奇妙，对吧？

304
00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:22.900
You can just go to any point.
你可以随时回到任何一个节点。

305
00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:37.780
I mean, for us, having made the show, I can recall things that I was experiencing when I see a scene or if I catch a chunk of an episode,
我是说，对我们而言，作为这部剧的创作者，当我看到某个场景或偶然看到某一集片段时，我能回想起当时自己的经历，

306
00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:42.380
I can remember where I was in my life a little bit more.
我能更清楚地记得当时自己处于人生的哪个阶段。

307
00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.620
And that is so true.
这话说得太对了。

308
00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:46.820
It's really wild.
真的很奇妙。

309
00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:53.340
And, you know, and like I said, even the ups and downs of it, it's all beautiful, right?
而且，就像我说的，即使是其中的起起落落，一切都很美好，对吧？

310
00:15:53.500 --> 00:16:04.720
And just to have those triggers available to remember those things, it feels very, yeah, it just feels very, very beautiful overall.
能有这些触发点来唤起那些记忆，感觉真的非常、非常美好。

311
00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:06.660
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

312
00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:12.600
This, as you were saying, is such a big storyline for you, traveling salesman leading into the return.
正如你所说，这对你来说是一条重要的故事线，从旅行推销员到回归。

313
00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:20.440
And were you super excited to get this script to see what all you got to do and play with Steve like you got to in this episode?
拿到剧本看到自己能做这么多事，还能像这一集里这样和史蒂夫演对手戏，你当时是不是特别兴奋？

314
00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:22.040
Yeah, of course.
是的，当然。

315
00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:25.520
I mean, it was still early enough for me.
我是说，那时对我来说还算早期阶段。

316
00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:29.400
I mean, every table read is a joy, right?
每次剧本围读都是一种享受，对吧？

317
00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.100
It's just so much discovery for the caSt.
对演员来说，那充满了发现的乐趣。

318
00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:37.340
The writers have been kind of in the trenches for a couple of weeks or months at that point with every episode.
编剧们在那之前已经为每一集在幕后苦思冥想了好几周甚至好几个月。

319
00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:40.600
But for us, it's like this fresh, exciting thing.
但对我们来说，这就像是一件新鲜、令人兴奋的事情。

320
00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:50.400
And I do remember feeling, you know, as I got more sort of meat on the bone in some of those later episodes,
我确实记得那种感觉，随着我在后面几集里有了更多发挥空间，

321
00:16:50.400 --> 00:17:03.640
it just felt – it really felt like an affirmation that Andy was a valuable part of this, you know, cast overall and of the show.
那感觉就像是对安迪作为整个剧组和这部剧重要一员的肯定。

322
00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:11.560
And so, yeah, there was always a kind of giddy anticipation with those table reads, like, oh, what am I going to get to do?
所以，是的，每次围读时总有一种兴奋的期待，心想：哦，我这次能做什么呢？

323
00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.260
What did the writers come up with, you know?
编剧们又想出了什么点子呢？

324
00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:18.600
And then, of course, what did the – how is Steve going to make me laugh this week?
当然还有，史蒂夫这周又会怎么逗我笑呢？

325
00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:19.340
Right.
没错。

326
00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.980
Ed, this episode opens with your phone ringing.
艾德，这一集开头是你的手机铃声响了。

327
00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:28.180
And there's, like, a four-part harmony of you singing.
里面有你唱的四部和声。

328
00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:28.940
Yeah.
是的。

329
00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:30.180
Your ringtone.
你的手机铃声。

330
00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:31.220
Is that you?
那是你唱的吗？

331
00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:32.380
100% me.
百分之百是我。

332
00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.140
All four parts?
四个声部都是？

333
00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:34.680
All of it?
全部吗？

334
00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:34.980
Oh, yeah.
哦，是的。

335
00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:35.480
Yeah.
是的。

336
00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:37.080
Yep.
没错。

337
00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:38.040
And I did it.
是我做的。

338
00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:44.420
And I – and just like Andy says in the show, I did it on my computer at home using –
而且——就像安迪在剧里说的那样，我是在家里用电脑做的——

339
00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.780
You, Ed, did it on Ed's computer at home?
你，艾德，是在艾德家里的电脑上做的？

340
00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:47.200
Yeah, I did.
是的，是我做的。

341
00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.720
They didn't, like, bring you into a studio or something?
他们没有把你带到录音棚之类的吗？

342
00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:52.420
No, I think they –
没有，我想他们——

343
00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:53.480
That tracks, actually.
这倒挺符合我们的风格。

344
00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.580
That does track for our show.
确实很符合我们剧组的作风。

345
00:17:55.720 --> 00:18:00.900
Like, hey, can you do this whole thing at home on your flip phone or whatever we had at the time?
就像是：嘿，你能用你的翻盖手机或者当时我们有的什么设备在家里搞定这一切吗？

346
00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:11.800
Well, I can't – I think maybe – I don't think they wanted it to be very good, but I was, like, too into it to make it anything less.
嗯，我不能——我想也许——我不觉得他们要求做得多好，但我当时太投入了，没法做得太差。

347
00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:14.700
And I thought Andy would make it as good as possible, right?
我觉得安迪会尽力把它做到最好，对吧？

348
00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:21.760
So, I think I spent a long time on that and GarageBand kind of figuring out how to do multiple tracks.
所以我花了很多时间在GarageBand上研究怎么做多轨录音。

349
00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:31.040
But, yeah, I recorded it and I sort of figured out four parts to it, which was really pretty simple.
不过，是的，我录了下来，还琢磨出了四个声部，其实挺简单的。

350
00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:41.020
I mean, it's a very elementary kind of harmony arrangement, but it – yeah, super fun.
我是说，那是一种非常基础的和声编排，但——是的，超级有趣。

351
00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:45.500
And then I played it for Greg and he was like, great, we'll use it.
然后我放给格雷格听，他说，太棒了，我们就用这个。

352
00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:48.680
I feel like, Ed, because I know you were in an acapella group, right, in college?
埃德，我觉得，因为我知道你大学时参加过阿卡贝拉合唱团，对吧？

353
00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:52.520
Well, yeah, that's a little bit of an overblown history.
嗯，对，那段经历有点被夸大了。

354
00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:56.760
I was in an acapella group, but only for a semester in college.
我确实参加过阿卡贝拉合唱团，但大学里只待了一个学期。

355
00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:03.040
It wasn't, like, a huge part of my identity, although it was for a semester, but then I just kind of realized it wasn't for me.
那并不是我身份中很重要的一部分，虽然确实待过一个学期，但后来我意识到那不适合我。

356
00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.220
But I had that.
不过我确实有过那段经历。

357
00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:06.280
But you have an ear.
但你有音乐天赋。

358
00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:07.680
You have an ear for music.
你对音乐很有耳感。

359
00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:10.840
Like, there is no way they could have given that assignment to Jen and I.
比如，他们绝对不可能把那个任务交给我和詹。

360
00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:12.300
I know.
我知道。

361
00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.500
You're like, it's a pretty easy harmony.
你会说，这和声挺简单的。

362
00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:15.700
And I'm like, is it?
而我会说，是吗？

363
00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:16.600
It is?
真的吗？

364
00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:16.980
Yeah.
是的。

365
00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:18.480
You're very talented.
你非常有才华。

366
00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:24.220
Well, since we're talking about famous Andy Bernard things, I need to ask you about –
好吧，既然我们在聊安迪·伯纳德的那些名场面，我得问你关于——

367
00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:32.300
I have the script and I have not once ever seen it written in the script for Andy Bernard.
我有剧本，但我从来没在安迪·伯纳德的剧本里看到过那句台词。

368
00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:34.140
You had to have invented this.
这肯定是你自己发明的。

369
00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:44.180
I improvised a ton in my early episodes because I – again, I was eager to prove myself and I also was trying to find Andy Bernard as a character.
我在早期剧集里即兴发挥了很多，因为——再说一次，我当时急于证明自己，也还在努力寻找安迪·伯纳德这个角色的定位。

370
00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:55.280
And that particular thing, which I think was – I think this was the first episode where it appeared for Andy, right?
而那个特定的梗，我想应该是——我想这是安迪第一次出现这个梗的剧集，对吧？

371
00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:55.680
Yes.
是的。

372
00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:56.000
Yes.
没错。

373
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:10.100
And so it just was this thing that these guys I went to middle school with would say – they would just inject into – it was kind of like a, there you go.
所以这只是我初中同学常说的一句话——他们会把它加在句子里——就像是一种“就是这样”的感觉。

374
00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:10.860
Gotcha.
懂了。

375
00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:16.900
You know, if they got off like a good zinger at your expense, they'd tag it with it.
你知道，如果他们损了你一句，让你觉得很尴尬，他们就会补上这一句。

376
00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:29.080
And I don't know if – they weren't exactly bullies, but I just remember this little clump of guys that were sort of like wisecrackers in my middle school.
我不知道——他们算不上是恶霸，但我记得初中时有那么一小群人，他们算是那种爱耍嘴皮子的人。

377
00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:34.080
And I don't know where it came from before that.
我也不知道在那之前这个梗是从哪来的。

378
00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:39.900
Picked it up and always thought it was kind of a funny, obnoxious expression.
我学会了之后，一直觉得这是一个既滑稽又讨厌的表达。

379
00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:46.040
It has no meaning and it just kind of fits in wherever you want.
它没有任何实际意义，但放在哪里都挺合适的。

380
00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.460
It's so perfect, Ed.
这太完美了，埃德。

381
00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.560
I have such a memory of being in Scranton with you, Ed.
埃德，我记忆中和你一起在斯克兰顿的经历非常深刻。

382
00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:58.560
Do you remember – I remember like we were there for the office convention and there were so many people there.
你还记得吗——我记得当时我们去参加《办公室》的粉丝见面会，那里人山人海。

383
00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:04.660
And you and I were in a car and like there were people all around the car and we went into that train shop and we had to exit out the back.
你和我当时在车里，车周围全是人，我们走进那家火车模型店，最后不得不从后门溜走。

384
00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:06.100
I mean, that's right.
没错，确实是这样。

385
00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:11.760
We were driving through downtown Scranton and I was like, oh, my God, it's a Lionel model train store.
我们开车经过斯克兰顿市中心，我当时说，天哪，那是一家莱昂内尔火车模型店。

386
00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:13.080
We have to go in there.
我们得进去看看。

387
00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:19.820
And I made the car stop and you got out with me and we went and people started to press up against the glass.
我让车停下，你跟我一起下车，我们进去后，人们开始挤向玻璃窗。

388
00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:20.440
People surrounded.
人们把我们围住了。

389
00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:23.280
And it was like – we were like Michael Jackson.
感觉就像——我们成了迈克尔·杰克逊。

390
00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:23.640
Oh, my gosh, you guys.
天哪，你们这些人。

391
00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:24.840
It was crazy.
太疯狂了。

392
00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:28.000
You said to me – they pulled a car around the back.
你当时对我说——他们把车开到了后门。

393
00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.340
We had to like exit out the back.
我们不得不从后门撤离。

394
00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.480
And the man that owned the model train store, he was like an elderly gentleman.
店主是一位上了年纪的老先生。

395
00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:34.980
He had no idea who we were or what was happening.
他根本不知道我们是谁，也不知道发生了什么。

396
00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:38.100
He was like, I guess you can use the back door, whatever.
他当时说，好吧，你们可以用后门，随便吧。

397
00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:44.460
And then we got in the car and you're like, Ange, this is the closest we'll ever be to being a Beatle.
然后我们上了车，你对我说，安吉，这是我们离披头士乐队最近的一次了。

398
00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:46.900
Like this is our moment.
这简直是我们的高光时刻。

399
00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:52.980
But why I bring it up is that to a crowd of people, Ed just turned to them and said,
但我提起这件事是因为，面对那群人，埃德转过身对他们说了句，

400
00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:56.680
and they lost their minds.
然后他们就彻底疯狂了。

401
00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:58.640
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

402
00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:08.600
That's still – like if someone is kind of trailing me in an airport like, hey, Ed Elms, hey, Andy, Andy, what's up?
那还是——就像如果有人在机场跟着我，嘿，Ed Elms，嘿，Andy，Andy，怎么了？

403
00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:13.940
I'll just toss a rid of the doo-doo and then they're like – and they'll leave me alone.
我只要扔点排泄物过去，他们就会——他们就会离我远点。

404
00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:14.780
And then they faint.
然后他们就晕过去了。

405
00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:25.300
Well, Ed, I wanted to ask you about the scene where you punch the wall because I rewatched this with my kids.
好吧，Ed，我想问你关于你打墙的那场戏，因为我和孩子们重温了这一集。

406
00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:29.540
My kids are older now, Ed, and they love The Office and they love Andy.
我的孩子们现在长大了，Ed，他们很喜欢《办公室》，也很喜欢Andy。

407
00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:34.900
And we curled up in bed and we watched this episode and they were giggling so hard.
我们蜷在床上看这一集，他们笑得前仰后合。

408
00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:37.180
And they wanted to know anything about it.
他们想知道关于那场戏的任何细节。

409
00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:39.100
Like, do you have any story about punching the wall?
比如，你有什么关于打墙的故事吗？

410
00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:40.460
Did you really do it?
你是真的打了吗？

411
00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:41.780
What was it like?
那是什么感觉？

412
00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:44.320
Like, I know Jenna had a front-row seat at reception.
我知道Jenna当时在前台有绝佳的视角。

413
00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:44.980
Yeah.
是啊。

414
00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:47.400
Accounting watched it from afar.
会计部的人是在远处看的。

415
00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.740
Well, it's funny because that was technically a stunt, right?
嗯，很有趣，因为从技术上讲那是个特技，对吧？

416
00:22:50.920 --> 00:23:00.260
In like a production – from a production standpoint, the sort of stunt effects people have to oversee something like that.
在制作层面——从制作的角度来看，那种特技效果人员必须监督类似的事情。

417
00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:05.220
And so I remember it was my first time doing anything that qualified as a stunt.
所以我记得那是我第一次做任何被称为特技的事情。

418
00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:14.380
So, like, there was a whole little meeting about how they were going to score the back of the sheet rock so that my hand went through easier.
所以，我们开了一个小会，讨论他们要怎么在石膏板背面刻痕，好让我的手更容易穿过去。

419
00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:25.360
And then they were going to put a – they, like, fixed a pad onto the inner wall so that when my hand went through, I wouldn't hit, you know, wood or aluminum or anything.
然后他们打算放一个——他们固定了一个垫子在内墙上，这样当我的手穿过去时，我就不会打到木头、铝材之类的东西。

420
00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:31.380
And also they had multiple planks that they could stick up just so we could do multiple takes.
而且他们准备了多块板子，可以随时换上去，这样我们就能多拍几次。

421
00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:45.840
But it's still – I mean, you guys know anytime you as a character are holding court for the whole bullpen, it's daunting, right?
但那依然——我是说，你们都知道，无论何时，只要你作为一个角色在整个办公区里掌控全场，那都是很吓人的，对吧？

422
00:23:45.840 --> 00:24:00.020
It's kind of something that I think was – I always felt anxious about in a good way, like in a kind of – you know, I was always amped up for those kinds of things.
我觉得那是一种——我总是会感到紧张，但那是种好的紧张，就像——你知道，我总是对那种场面感到兴奋。

423
00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:14.940
It's also what makes me just stay in awe of Carell because so many of his scenes were with the entire cast where he's, like, running a scene with the entire caSt.
这也是为什么我一直对Carell感到敬畏，因为他的很多戏都是和全体演员一起演的，他就像是在带着整个剧组演戏。

424
00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.400
And that's so hard.
那太难了。

425
00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:17.860
I mean,
我是说，

426
00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:36.420
that is just – it is a really – it takes a tremendous amount of focus and concentration because everyone's lines are responding off of yours as opposed to – and so you have to drive the scene as opposed to being someone who's just responding to somebody else who's – which is what we all got to do with Steve.
那真的——那需要极大的专注力和集中力，因为每个人的台词都是在回应你的台词，而不是——所以你必须主导这场戏，而不是仅仅回应别人——而这正是我们所有人与Steve合作时所做的事情。

427
00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:48.020
So that was one of those scenes for me and a very early one and something that I think was very exciting but also kind of daunting.
所以对我来说，那是其中一场戏，而且是很早的一场，我认为那非常令人兴奋，但也确实有点吓人。

428
00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.440
And I really didn't want to screw up the punch.
我真的不想搞砸那一拳。

429
00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:58.700
I didn't want to get it wrong or there were a lot of kind of windups to practice swings, if you will.
我不想弄错，而且当时有很多预备动作来练习挥拳，如果你愿意这么说的话。

430
00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:08.740
Well, and then I think we only did two takes or so, and I skinned up my knuckles.
嗯，然后我想我们只拍了两条左右，我的指关节就擦破了。

431
00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:17.420
I do remember, you know, I got – I still – there was a pad in there, but I still, I think, breaking through the sheetrock skinned up my knuckles.
我确实记得，你知道，我——我还是——里面虽然有垫子，但我认为，打破石膏板还是擦破了我的指关节。

432
00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:19.580
So, yeah, that was pretty intense.
所以，是的，那相当激烈。

433
00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.820
I remember you shed some blood for that one.
我记得你为那场戏流了血。

434
00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:24.060
Yeah, tiny.
是的，一点点。

435
00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:25.780
And I remember that.
我也记得那个。

436
00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:36.440
But I also remember feeling as a person who was in the bullpen watching how I could not break because I knew the pressure you were under.
但我还记得作为一个在办公区观看的人，我当时在想我怎么才能不笑场，因为我知道你承受的压力。

437
00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:40.640
And I also knew we didn't have that many of the walls.
而且我也知道我们没有那么多墙板。

438
00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.060
They had extra, but it's not like they had 14.
他们有备用的，但也不是说有14块那么多。

439
00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:46.740
So we couldn't crack up when you punched the wall.
所以当你打墙的时候，我们不能笑场。

440
00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:48.220
We had to hold ourselves together.
我们必须克制住自己。

441
00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:53.280
So I remember it being like, oh, boy, all right, bite the inside of your lip.
所以我记得当时的感觉是，天哪，好吧，咬住嘴唇内侧。

442
00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:56.460
Whatever you have to do, do not mess this up.
无论你必须做什么，千万别搞砸了。

443
00:25:57.440 --> 00:25:58.360
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

444
00:25:58.460 --> 00:26:05.400
There's so many of those moments of trying to not look at what's happening because it's too funny.
有太多这样的时刻，试图不去看正在发生的事情，因为那太搞笑了。

445
00:26:06.820 --> 00:26:07.300
Yeah.
是啊。

446
00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.560
I can just remember like –
我只记得就像——

447
00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:11.240
Yeah, I feel like that was most of our day.
是啊，我觉得我们大部分时间都是这样。

448
00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:21.980
If I were in the bullpen for a scene like that and didn't want to laugh, I would just be looking at the wall three feet to the right just because that was my trick.
如果我在办公区拍那样的戏，不想笑的话，我就会盯着右边三英尺处的墙看，因为那是我的诀窍。

449
00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:27.000
I always just looked somewhere else because everyone's just so damn funny.
我总是看向别处，因为每个人都太该死的好笑了。

450
00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.940
I used to look at Steve's neck.
我以前总是盯着Steve的脖子看。

451
00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:29.560
I know.
我知道。

452
00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:30.460
That's so weird.
太奇怪了。

453
00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:30.740
Yeah.
是啊。

454
00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.420
I looked at his ear a lot.
我盯着他的耳朵看了好久。

455
00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:34.600
But I love that you were looking at his ear.
但我喜欢你盯着他耳朵看的样子。

456
00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:36.000
I was looking at his neck.
我当时在看他的脖子。

457
00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:36.440
Yeah.
是啊。

458
00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:38.540
I would usually look at like his forehead.
我通常会盯着他的额头看。

459
00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:41.880
These are the parts of Steve who did so well.
这些都是史蒂夫表现得非常出色的地方。

460
00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:42.160
Yeah.
是啊。

461
00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:43.620
I can tell you all about his ear.
我可以跟你讲讲他耳朵的所有细节。

462
00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:47.180
Steve right now is like, oh, good Lord.
史蒂夫现在肯定在想，天哪。

463
00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:52.000
There's something like so much of his – of Michael Scott was in those eyes.
他的眼神里有太多迈克尔·斯科特的影子了。

464
00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.960
And if you locked in on him, you're done.
一旦你跟他对上眼，你就完了。

465
00:26:55.320 --> 00:27:02.260
Just like all of the tsunami of comedy that is Michael Scott would just like wash over you.
迈克尔·斯科特那种喜剧海啸会瞬间把你淹没。

466
00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:06.700
And I could almost never keep it together with him.
和他演对手戏时，我几乎从没忍住不笑场。

467
00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:10.040
Even just the dumbest little moments, he just –
哪怕是最愚蠢的小瞬间，他只要……

468
00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.040
Well, you guys had so many great moments in this episode.
你们这一集里确实有很多精彩的瞬间。

469
00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:19.360
Like I love when you're in his office like telling him about like I'm going to get my beer on, get my lost on.
比如我特别喜欢你在他办公室里跟他说你要去喝啤酒、去放纵一下的那段。

470
00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:21.760
And you follow him to the bathroom.
然后你还跟着他去了洗手间。

471
00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:22.320
Yeah.
是啊。

472
00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:23.520
What was that like?
那是什么感觉？

473
00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:24.740
I'm on my way to the kitchen.
我要去厨房了。

474
00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:25.540
I'll follow you.
我跟着你。

475
00:27:25.780 --> 00:27:26.620
I'll walk you there.
我陪你走过去。

476
00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:27.560
Yeah.
是啊。

477
00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:29.240
Oh, gosh.
天哪。

478
00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:29.780
Yeah.
是啊。

479
00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:39.280
So, I mean, the writers just – you know, there was something – a few of the writers and Mike Schur in particular,
所以，我的意思是，编剧们……你知道，有些编剧，特别是迈克·舒尔，

480
00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:49.460
we really bonded at that time because for some reason, Mike Schur just got Andy Bernard in this really weird way.
我们当时真的建立了深厚的感情，因为不知为何，迈克·舒尔就是能以一种非常奇特的方式理解安迪·伯纳德。

481
00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:55.040
I mean, maybe there's some bit of Andy Bernard in Michael Schur as well.
我是说，也许迈克·舒尔本人身上也有一点安迪·伯纳德的影子。

482
00:27:55.200 --> 00:28:02.160
But, I mean, the stuff they came up with for him, I would read these scripts or, you know, be at the table and I'd be like,
但他们为他创作的内容，我每次读剧本或者在围读会上，我都会觉得，

483
00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:04.780
yes, this is exactly what Andy would do.
没错，这就是安迪会做的事。

484
00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:11.100
This is exactly how the Andy in my head, the way that I think about him, would behave.
这就是我脑海中安迪的行为方式，完全符合我对他的理解。

485
00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:13.100
And so, I just got it.
所以我完全能领会。

486
00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:19.100
I felt like these scenes were like such a perfect fit, like putting on a nice comfy sweater.
我觉得这些场景非常契合，就像穿上一件舒适的毛衣一样自然。

487
00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:19.480
I mean,
我是说，

488
00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:34.520
you guys know the writers paid such close attention to the actors that they've – and it's that great thing that happens around late in season two or season three of a show or even earlier than that
你们都知道编剧们非常关注演员，这在剧集第二季末或第三季，甚至更早的时候就会发生，

489
00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:49.400
where the writers start to write towards the strengths of the actors or towards certain personality traits or mannerisms or all – just these incredibly specific things that start to bubble up.
编剧们开始根据演员的优势、特定的性格特征、习惯动作，或者那些开始涌现的极其具体的细节来创作。

490
00:28:52.500 --> 00:29:02.660
And Mike Schur and I would just sit around and laugh and like come up with bits about who Andy was and his background and his family life and just die laughing.
我和迈克·舒尔经常坐在一起大笑，构思关于安迪的背景、家庭生活之类的桥段，笑得停不下来。

491
00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:15.240
And we just had such a common understanding of the character that things like that, walking Michael to the bathroom, which is so insane,
我们对这个角色有着高度共识，所以像跟着迈克去洗手间这种疯狂的举动，

492
00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:22.060
but it made perfect sense to me for Andy and was just a joy to do.
对我来说却完全符合安迪的逻辑，演起来特别开心。

493
00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:30.580
Well, I can tell you, Ed, that the cast, when we got to see you be in scenes, when you finally came, you know, because we didn't see you guys.
艾德，我得告诉你，当演员们看到你出现在场景里时，当你终于加入我们的时候，因为之前我们没见过你们。

494
00:29:30.580 --> 00:29:34.280
You guys were over at Stanford and we didn't get to interact with you.
你们当时在斯坦福分部，我们没机会和你们互动。

495
00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:41.360
And when we finally got to see you in scenes, you just crushed every scene you were in and you were so funny.
当我们终于看到你演戏时，你简直演活了每一个场景，太搞笑了。

496
00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.960
And I remember trying so hard not to laugh.
我记得我当时拼命忍住不笑。

497
00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:47.380
It used to be my goal, don't laugh when Steve does something.
我以前的目标就是，史蒂夫演戏时千万别笑。

498
00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.480
Don't laugh when Steve does something.
史蒂夫演戏时千万别笑。

499
00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:52.580
And then all of a sudden I had to add this other layer of like, now don't laugh when Ed does something.
结果突然之间，我又得加上一条：现在艾德演戏时也千万别笑。

500
00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:53.240
Oh, my God.
天哪。

501
00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:05.000
Michael's trying to play a jukebox, but he can't start the tune.
迈克尔想放点唱机，但怎么也放不出歌来。

502
00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:09.460
So Andy starts doing the Night at the Roxbury song.
于是安迪开始跳《舞翻天》里的那个动作。

503
00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:12.340
That is in, is that in the merger?
那是在……那是合并案里的内容吗？

504
00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:13.940
Yeah, I think so.
对，我想是的。

505
00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:14.320
I think.
我想应该是。

506
00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:24.560
Peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep.
哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔。

507
00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:27.460
What is love?
什么是爱？

508
00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.180
Our trailers were side by side.
我们的拖车房是挨在一起的。

509
00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:33.440
So you were the one, you know, I just walked out my door and went around the corner and there you were.
所以你就是那个……你知道，我一出门转个弯，你就站在那儿。

510
00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:37.640
And I know I have this memory of walking past your trailer because you would have the door open.
我记得我走过你拖车房的时候，因为你总是开着门。

511
00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.060
You'd be playing your banjo or something.
你当时在弹班卓琴还是什么的。

512
00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:43.980
And I would walk past your door and go, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep, peep.
我走过你门口时就会发出，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔，哔。

513
00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:46.120
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

514
00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:52.180
Well, I loved the addition of Andy because you were this great foil to Dwight.
说真的，我很喜欢安迪这个角色的加入，因为你是德怀特绝佳的对手。

515
00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:55.740
Like Jim would always tease Dwight or prank Dwight.
就像吉姆总是捉弄或恶搞德怀特一样。

516
00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:58.960
You had this like power struggle with Dwight
你和德怀特之间那种权力斗争，

517
00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:00.880
that was so funny to me.
我觉得特别好笑。

518
00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:02.060
And in rewatching it,
重温这部剧的时候，

519
00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:06.840
I just love every time you and Rain would face off.
我特别喜欢看你和雷恩针锋相对的每一场戏。

520
00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:09.800
And there must've been so much improv in that
那里面肯定有很多即兴发挥，

521
00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:13.320
because I feel like now having the scripts,
因为我觉得现在有了剧本，

522
00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:14.600
when I go back and look,
当我回头去看的时候，

523
00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:17.240
I can see now percentage-wise
我能看出百分比了，

524
00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:20.500
how much more of different people's improvs
到底有多少不同演员的即兴表演，

525
00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:21.460
make it in the show.
最终被剪进了正片里。

526
00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:23.660
And I have to say that you and Rain Wilson
我不得不说，你和雷恩·威尔森，

527
00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:27.320
have the highest amount of improv that makes the cut.
是即兴表演被采用率最高的两位。

528
00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:29.460
Wow, that's cool.
哇，那太酷了。

529
00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:31.920
Well, until Zach Woods joins the cast
不过，直到扎克·伍兹加入剧组，

530
00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:36.880
and then Zach Woods just demolished the improv.
扎克·伍兹简直是即兴表演的终结者。

531
00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:40.480
He is a genius and he's so smart.
他是个天才，而且非常聪明。

532
00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:41.880
So, so smart and funny.
非常、非常聪明又幽默。

533
00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:45.220
But yeah, Jenna, it's funny, you're right.
不过没错，珍娜，你说得对，挺有意思的。

534
00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:50.680
I think the Jim-Dwight dynamic was hilarious
我觉得吉姆和德怀特的互动非常搞笑，

535
00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:56.580
because Jim was a reasonable person
因为吉姆是个理智的人，

536
00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:01.540
and Dwight is a sort of whacked out, unreasonable person.
而德怀特是个有点疯癫、不讲理的人。

537
00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:06.820
And seeing Jim always in a sort of superior role,
看到吉姆总是处于一种优越的地位，

538
00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:11.460
just messing with Dwight was fun and gratifying to see.
去捉弄德怀特，既有趣又让人感到满足。

539
00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:14.300
You know, Andy was also whacked out.
你知道，安迪也挺疯的。

540
00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:18.080
And so he really was at Dwight's level.
所以他真的和德怀特处于同一个水平线上。

541
00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:22.380
And that, I think, made their conflict super fun.
我觉得这让他们的冲突变得超级有趣。

542
00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:25.320
And certainly super fun for Rain and me.
对我和雷恩来说当然也超级有趣。

543
00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:30.580
I just can, yeah, so many hard laughs with that guy.
我真的……没错，和那家伙在一起总是能笑得前仰后合。

544
00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:33.800
And you guys would compete over the stupidest things.
你们俩会为了最愚蠢的事情争个高下。

545
00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:37.400
I mean, I know it's not in this episode, but there's a deleted scene
我是说，我知道这一集里没有，但有个删减片段，

546
00:32:37.400 --> 00:32:42.700
where you two, like, are going head to head about who was the better baby.
你们俩在那儿针锋相对，争论谁才是更好的婴儿。

547
00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:46.760
Like, you're in the kitchen and you're like, well, I was a beautiful baby.
就像你在厨房里说，好吧，我可是个漂亮的婴儿。

548
00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:47.280
I was a model.
我当过模特。

549
00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:48.420
So I was a model baby.
所以我可是个模特婴儿。

550
00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:49.060
I modeled.
我做过模特。

551
00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:51.080
And then he's like, well, I was so big.
然后他就说，哎呀，我当时长得可大了。

552
00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:52.300
My mom couldn't walk.
我妈都走不动路了。

553
00:32:52.460 --> 00:32:53.320
And you're like, gross.
你就会觉得，真恶心。

554
00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:57.820
Like, it's, but it's like this crazy competition over the stupidest thing.
就像是，这简直是在为最蠢的事情进行疯狂的攀比。

555
00:32:57.820 --> 00:33:02.380
Yeah, but you're competing over who was the better baby, but your standards for what better
没错，你们在争论谁才是更好的婴儿，但你们对于“更好”的

556
00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:04.240
baby means aren't even the same.
定义根本就不一样。

557
00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:08.320
Like, you're not arguing over who was most beautiful or who was largeSt.
你们争的又不是谁最漂亮或者谁个头最大。

558
00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:11.100
It's like this complicated one-upmanship.
这就像是一种复杂的争强好胜。

559
00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:11.940
And I love it.
但我喜欢这样。

560
00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:16.140
Oh, so, such flawed characters.
哦，真是的，全是这种有缺陷的角色。

561
00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:19.280
Deliciously flawed.
妙不可言的缺陷。

562
00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:26.320
And I think, you know, the pettiness of Andy was a bit cathartic for me.
而且我觉得，安迪的那种小家子气对我来说其实挺有宣泄作用的。

563
00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:33.340
Because, you know, it just, it was a way, I was saying, we all have petty reactions and
